public class CasePriorityHandler {
    public static void handle(List<Case> newCases) {
        // Collect all account IDs from new cases to avoid N+1 SOQL queries
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        // Query all relevant account ratings in a single SOQL query
        Map<Id, String> accountRatings = new Map<Id, String>();
        if (!accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (Account acc : [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]) {
                accountRatings.put(acc.Id, acc.Rating);
            }
        }

        // Assign priority based on account rating; leave other cases unchanged
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null && accountRatings.get(c.AccountId) == 'Hot') {
                c.Priority = 'High';
            }
            // Cases without an account or with a non-Hot rating keep the Priority they were created with
        }
    }
}
```

**What changed and why:**  
The original implementation queried `Account.Rating` inside the `for` loop (`[SELECT Rating FROM Account WHERE Id = :c.AccountId]`` for every case). While this is indeed easy to read, it causes an N+1 SOQL problem: 200 cases from the ERP integration would execute 200 separate queries, instantly hitting the governor limit of 100 SOQL queries in Apex. The fix collects all account IDs upfront, performs **one** SOQL query, stores the results in a `Map<Id, String>`, and then applies the priority logic in a single loop. This keeps the code simple and maintainable for admins, respects bulk batches of up to 200, and avoids the `Too many SOQL queries: 101` limit exception. The functional result is identical: cases with a `Hot` account rating get `High` priority; every other case (including those without an account) retains its original priority.

